          <h2>Scheduling Criteria</h2>
          <p>Schedulers are evaluated against competing, often conflicting objectives:</p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>CPU utilization</strong> — keep the processor as busy as possible; target 40–90% under load.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Throughput</strong> — number of processes completed per unit time.</li>
            <li><strong>Turnaround time</strong> — total elapsed time from submission to completion.</li>
            <li><strong>Waiting time</strong> — cumulative time a process spends in the ready queue.</li>
            <li><strong>Response time</strong> — time from request submission to first output; critical for interactive
              systems.</li>
          </ul>

          <h2>Scheduling Algorithms</h2>

          <h3>First-Come, First-Served (FCFS)</h3>
          <p>
            Processes are dispatched in arrival order, implemented as a simple FIFO queue.
            Easy to reason about, but susceptible to the <span class="term">convoy effect</span> —
            a long CPU-bound process can stall many shorter I/O-bound processes behind it.
          </p>
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Process</th>
                <th>Burst Time</th>
                <th>Waiting Time</th>
                <th>Turnaround</th>
