              <tr>
                <td><code>P1</code></td>
                <td>24 ms</td>
                <td>0 ms</td>
                <td>24 ms</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>P2</code></td>
                <td>3 ms</td>
                <td>24 ms</td>
                <td>27 ms</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td><code>P3</code></td>
                <td>3 ms</td>
                <td>27 ms</td>
                <td>30 ms</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
          <p>Avg. wait = <code>(0 + 24 + 27) / 3 = 17 ms</code>. Reordering P2 first drops this to <code>3 ms</code> —
            arrival order alone determines performance.</p>

          <h3>Shortest Job First (SJF)</h3>
          <p>
            Selects the process with the smallest next CPU burst. Provably <strong>optimal for minimizing average
              waiting time</strong> when all burst lengths are known in advance.
            In practice, burst length must be estimated via <span class="term">exponential averaging</span>:
          </p>


          <h3>Round-Robin (RR)</h3>
          <p>
            Each process receives a fixed <span class="term">time quantum</span> <em>q</em> (typically 10–100 ms).
            Once the quantum expires, the process is preempted and added to the tail of the ready queue.
            Designed for time-sharing systems with strong response-time requirements.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>If <em>q</em> is very large → degenerates to FCFS with no preemption.</li>
            <li>If <em>q</em> is very small → context-switch overhead dominates CPU time.</li>
            <li>Rule of thumb: 80% of CPU bursts should be shorter than <em>q</em>.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="callout-title">Context Switch Cost</span>
            <p>A context switch takes roughly 1–10 µs on modern hardware. With a 1 ms quantum, that's up to 1% of CPU
              time spent purely in switching — quantum sizing is a real engineering trade-off.</p>
          </div>

          <h3>Priority Scheduling</h3>
          <p>
            The CPU is given to the highest-priority ready process. Can operate in preemptive or non-preemptive mode.
            The core risk is <strong>indefinite blocking</strong> (starvation) of low-priority processes.
          </p>
          <p>
            The standard mitigation is <span class="term">aging</span>: periodically increase the priority of
            long-waiting processes.
            A process waiting 15 minutes might gain +1 priority every 5 minutes until it runs.
          </p>

          <h3>Completely Fair Scheduler (CFS)</h3>
          <p>
            Linux's default scheduler since kernel 2.6.23. Rather than tracking time slices, CFS tracks
            <span class="term">virtual runtime</span> (<code>vruntime</code>) — CPU time consumed, weighted
            inversely by <code>nice</code> priority. The scheduler always runs the process with the smallest
            <code>vruntime</code>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ready processes are stored in a <strong>red-black tree</strong> ordered by <code>vruntime</code>.
            The next process to run is always the leftmost node — O(log <em>n</em>) insert, O(1) selection.
            No process starves: every process's <code>vruntime</code> increases monotonically.
          </p>

          <h2>Context Switching</h2>
          <p>
            A context switch saves the full state of the outgoing process into its
            <span class="term">Process Control Block (PCB)</span>, then restores the state of the incoming process.
            The PCB records:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>CPU registers: PC, SP, general-purpose registers, condition codes</li>
            <li>Process state: running, ready, waiting, or terminated</li>
            <li>Scheduling metadata: priority, queue pointers, accounting data</li>
            <li>Memory context: page table base register, segment descriptors</li>
            <li>I/O state: open file descriptors, pending I/O operations</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="callout">
            <span class="callout-title">Mode Switch vs. Context Switch</span>
            <p>A <em>mode switch</em> (user → kernel) changes privilege level but keeps the same process's context in
              place. A <em>context switch</em> replaces one process's state entirely with another's — a strictly heavier
              operation.</p>
          </div>

